index bc7c5cb..018c521 100644
--- a/doc/upmpdcli-manual.txt
+++ b/doc/upmpdcli-manual.txt
@@ -1019,6 +1019,36 @@ The new connection method needs an initial user interaction:
   server, which will save them to a cached file and should then produce a success page in the
   browser. 
 
+Pay close attention to which upmpdcli process receives the browser redirect. The
+`qobuz-init-oauth.py` script only prints the URL. The token is saved by the already-running
+upmpdcli process when the browser redirects to `/qobuz/oauth/`.
+
+With the usual Linux system service, upmpdcli starts as root, drops privileges to the `upmpdcli`
+user, and uses `/var/cache/upmpdcli` unless `cachedir` is set in the configuration file. In this
+case the Qobuz token must end up in:
+
+----
+/var/cache/upmpdcli/qobuz/config
+----
+
+If you stop the service and run upmpdcli manually as your login user, the
+token will instead be written under that user's home directory:
+
+----
+~/.cache/upmpdcli/qobuz/config
+----
+
+This can make the manual instance work while the real boot-time service still fails to log in. To
+avoid this, authenticate against the same instance which will normally run as the service, or set an
+explicit `cachedir` in `upmpdcli.conf` and use it consistently for both the temporary/manual instance
+and the service. If you already authenticated with a manual instance, copy the generated
+`qobuz/config` file into the service cache directory and make it readable and writable by the
+`upmpdcli` service user.
+
+Also check the URL choice printed by `qobuz-init-oauth.py`: if the browser runs on a different
+machine from upmpdcli, do not use the `localhost` URL, because this would redirect the browser to
+the browser machine itself instead of to the upmpdcli host.
+
 This operation is only performed once, or at least very rarely.
 
 Additional configuration parameters are described in the <<qobuzuser,configuration section>>
